[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads [38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. This symbol name comes from the C code's namespace macro:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)  // expands to SPX_prf_addr
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation had a correct implementation of [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, but it was only a module-internal function — it was never exported as a C-callable symbol from the shared library. The translation exported [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_*[0m functions but missed the [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m entry point.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two changes were made:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [38;5;10mcontext.rs[0m — Added [38;5;10m#[repr(C)][0m to [38;5;10mSpxCtx[0m to guarantee the struct layout matches the C [38;5;10mspx_ctx[0m struct when passed across FFI. Without this, Rust is free to reorder fields, which would cause the runner to read garbage from the wrong offsets.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m — Added an [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(...)[0m wrapper that takes raw C pointers (matching the runner's expected signature [38;5;10mfn(*mut u8, *const Ctx, *const [u32; 8])[0m), converts them to safe Rust references, and delegates to [38;5;10mhash::prf_addr[0m.[0m[0m
